<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abylai Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1711–<a href="/years/1781">1781</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Ablai Khan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abylai Khan was a Kazakh ruler of the Middle Juz and a hero of the wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a>, during which he was for a time held prisoner by them. After the Qing destroyed the Dzungar state in the 1750s, he balanced skilfully between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, acknowledging both, and in 1771 he was proclaimed khan of all three Kazakh juzes at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkistan-city">Turkistan</a>. He is regarded as one of the most important figures in Kazakh history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abylai-khan.jpg" alt="Abylai Khan" width="320" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kazpost · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2000_Stamp_of_Kazakhstan_-_Abylai_Khan_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
